\ Dogfood: `core::cmp`'s `Ord` surface, exercised through `hosted::testing`.
\ `expect-eq` does real work here (R5.1) for the two scalar widths this
\ suite exercises (`i64`, `f64`); the remaining comparisons return a bare
\ `Bool`, so they go through `expect` instead (`Bool` has no `Ord` impl of
\ its own), with a false answer asserted by inverting the arms the way
\ `bool.sth` does.
\
\ Each comparison is probed at all three `Ordering` positions (`2 3` is
\ `Less`, `2 2` is `Equal`, `3 2` is `Greater`), since every one of them is
\ three arms over `cmp`. One probe per operator lands where that operator
\ agrees with a neighbour: an equal-operand probe cannot separate `lte` from
\ `eq`, and an unequal one cannot separate `ne` from `lt`.

import: intrinsics * ;
import: core::prelude * ;
import: hosted::testing | expect expect-eq | ;

: main ( -- )
  2 2 "eq holds for equal i64 values" expect-eq
  1 1 add 2 "eq checks real arithmetic, not just repeated literals" expect-eq
  2.0 2.0 "eq holds for equal f64 values" expect-eq

  2 3 eq ~[ False ] ~[ True ] if "eq fails when the left operand is smaller" expect
  3 2 eq ~[ False ] ~[ True ] if "eq fails when the left operand is larger" expect

  2 3 lt "lt holds when the left operand is smaller" expect
  2 2 lt ~[ False ] ~[ True ] if "lt fails for equal operands" expect
  3 2 lt ~[ False ] ~[ True ] if "lt fails when the left operand is larger" expect

  2 3 gt ~[ False ] ~[ True ] if "gt fails when the left operand is smaller" expect
  2 2 gt ~[ False ] ~[ True ] if "gt fails for equal operands" expect
  3 2 gt "gt holds when the left operand is larger" expect

  2 3 lte "lte holds when the left operand is smaller" expect
  2 2 lte "lte holds for equal operands" expect
  3 2 lte ~[ False ] ~[ True ] if "lte fails when the left operand is larger" expect

  2 3 gte ~[ False ] ~[ True ] if "gte fails when the left operand is smaller" expect
  2 2 gte "gte holds for equal operands" expect
  3 2 gte "gte holds when the left operand is larger" expect

  2 3 ne "ne holds when the left operand is smaller" expect
  2 2 ne ~[ False ] ~[ True ] if "ne fails for equal operands" expect
  3 2 ne "ne holds when the left operand is larger" expect ;
